What is the output of the following C program?

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    double a[2] = {20.0, 25.0};
    double *p, *q;
    p = a;
    q = p + 1;
    printf("%d,%d", (int)(q - p), (int)(*q - *p));
    return 0;
}
```

Let X be an array of n positive integers. A subarray of X is a sequence of array locations with consecutive indices. The following C code computes the length of the longest subarray containing at most two distinct integers. The code has two missing expressions, (P) and (Q):

```c
int first = 0, second = 0, len1 = 0, len2 = 0, maxlen = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (X[i] == first) {
        len2++;
        len1++;
    } else if (X[i] == second) {
        len2++;
        len1 = (P);
        second = first;
    } else {
        len2 = (Q);
        len1 = 1;
        second = first;
    }
    if (len2 > maxlen) {
        maxlen = len2;
    }
    first = X[i];
}
```

Which option gives the correct missing expressions?

Hint: At the end of iteration i, len1 is the length of the longest subarray ending at X[i] that contains only equal values, and len2 is the length of the longest subarray ending at X[i] that contains at most two distinct values.

Consider a multithreaded program with two threads, T1 and T2. They share semaphores s1 (initialized to 1) and s2 (initialized to 0), and a global variable x (initialized to 0). The threads execute the following code.

Thread T1:
```text
wait(s1);
x = x + 1;
print(x);
wait(s2);
signal(s1);
```

Thread T2:
```text
wait(s1);
x = x + 1;
print(x);
signal(s2);
signal(s1);
```

Which outcomes are possible when T1 and T2 execute concurrently?

